<LegalLayout
  title="Affiliate Disclosure"
  description="How The Hydration Code makes money: affiliate commissions on product links, disclosed in full and in plain language."
>
  <p>
    <strong
      >The Hydration Code earns a commission when you buy a product through a link on this
      site, at no additional cost to you.</strong
    > That's the entire business model — there is no other revenue source, no sponsored placements,
    and no bottle appears in a review because a brand paid for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Which programs</h2>
  <p>
    Product links on this site are built to route through the <strong>Amazon Associates
    Program</strong>, an affiliate advertising program designed to provide a means for sites to earn
    commission fees by linking to Amazon. Every affiliate link — including every "Check price ↗"
    button — points at a cloaked <code>/go/</code> redirect that carries the tracking tag for
    whichever program applies, so no raw retailer link ever appears directly on the page.
  </p>
  <p>
    We're building an audience before applying for the Associates account itself, which is standard
    practice and, in Amazon's case, a requirement — new applicants need a minimum of original content
    and traffic before approval, and only get one clean 180-day window to complete a set number of
    qualifying sales once accepted. Until that account is approved and active, our Amazon links may not
    yet be earning a commission even though the mechanism described above is fully in place. This page
    will be updated the moment that changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    As we expand coverage to specific brands sold directly by their manufacturers, we may also join
    programs like ShareASale or Impact, or negotiate direct affiliate relationships with individual
    brands. Those typically pay a meaningfully higher commission rate than Amazon's — which is worth
    stating plainly, because it's exactly the kind of incentive a disclosure page exists to surface. It
    does not change which bottles we recommend; the review criteria on <a href="/our-method"
    >Our Method</a> apply regardless of which program, if any, a given link belongs to.
  </p>

  <h2>What this does and doesn't affect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not affect the price you pay. Affiliate commissions come out of the retailer's margin, not yours.</li>
    <li>It does not influence which bottles we choose to review, or what we say about them — including the downsides.</li>
    <li>Amazon's own rules prohibit us from displaying an Amazon price unless it's sourced live from their Product Advertising API. Until we have that access, Amazon product pages show "Check price ↗" instead of a dollar figure, by design, not oversight.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this disclosure appears</h2>
  <p>
    You'll see a short version of this disclosure above the first affiliate link on any page that has
    one, and in the site footer on every page. This is the full version.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    If anything here is unclear, or you think a review reads more like an advertisement than an
    evaluation, reach out through our <a href="/contact">contact form</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
